# Build context exclusions — the repo root is the context for `server/Dockerfile`, so WITHOUT this
# file every `docker build` ships the entire working tree to the daemon.
#
# That is not a tidiness issue. `target/` alone is ~153 GB on a developer machine, and Docker
# Desktop's VM disk is ~39 GB, so the build fills the VM and dies with
# `no space left on device` — and `docker compose build` STILL EXITS 0, so the failure looks like a
# successful no-op and the old image keeps running. That is exactly how the coordinator went on
# serving a 7-week-old binary while three "rebuilds" reported success.
#
# Nothing here is needed by any Dockerfile: `server/Dockerfile` copies `Cargo.lock`, `server/`,
# `lib/` and `Rocket.toml`, and builds inside the image.

# Rust build output — the big one.
target/
**/target/
**/*.rs.bk

# Version control and its history.
.git/
.gitignore

# Node / web client dependencies and builds.
node_modules/
**/node_modules/
**/dist/
**/build/

# Local databases, wallets and logs — never wanted in an image, and some contain keys.
*.db
*.db-journal
*.sqlite
*.log
rgb-data-*/

# Editor/OS noise.
.DS_Store
.vscode/
.idea/

# Docs and scratch: not used by any build, and the largest non-target directories.
docs/
*.md
